    <article class="doc">
        <h2 id="usage">Usage</h2>

        <pre><code>pkgdel &lt;options&gt; &lt;portname&gt;
pkgdel --verbose &lt;portname&gt;
pkgdel --root=/mnt &lt;portname&gt;
pkgdel --help</code></pre>

        <p>
            The <code>pkgdel</code> utility removes one installed package by
            consulting the local package database. Dependency and orphan
            handling belong to higher-level Emerald commands and are not
            performed by <code>pkgdel</code> itself.
        </p>

        <h2 id="workflow">Removal workflow</h2>

        <p>
            The package database is authoritative for installed files. A removal
            operation reads that list, preserves configuration files according
            to package metadata, removes owned files, updates the database, and
            runs cleanup triggers.
        </p>

        <pre><code>emerald files example
emerald dependent example
emerald remove example</code></pre>

        <h2 id="safety">Safety notes</h2>

        <p>
            Never remove a package merely because its directory looks unused.
            Check reverse dependencies first. Keep a recovery shell available
            when removing low-level libraries, shell packages, boot components,
            or packages needed to boot or administer the system.
        </p>

        <h2 id="help">
            <code>pkgdel help</code>
        </h2>

        <pre><code>Usage:
    pkgdel &lt;options&gt; &lt;portname&gt;

Options:
    -h, --help     show this help message
    -v, --verbose  print removed files

Global options:
    --root=&lt;path&gt;  remove package from custom root directory</code></pre>
    </article>
</section>
